session.text:
UID ids all get the same 404. The AC3 and AC4 tests in `security.test.js` check this.
- **DES-2:** Security no longer says TBD. It now specifies:
  - the `X-User` rules: exact regex with no trim, duplicate-header and comma rejection through `rawHeaders`, and 401 before any body is read;
  - the loopback default bind and the non-loopback startup warning;
  - the trust boundary at the gateway;
  - logging limited to method, route template, status and duration;
  - `Map`-only storage and the threat list.
- **DES-3:** I added the following:
  - a component table;
  - full contracts for the four endpoints plus the error table;
  - the `Map<user, Map<id, note>>` data design with the note schema;
  - `FileNoteRepository` writes through a temp file, fsync and atomic rename, serialized by a promise queue and rolled back on a failed flush;
  - startup refusal on a leftover temp file or a corrupt data file, without touching the file;
  - the 16 KB request cap and the 500-note cap;
  - a table mapping R1–R8 to components.
- **DES-4:** I replaced the manual testing plan with a `node:test` plan: `node --test test/` and `node test/perf.js`. An AC1–AC11 traceability matrix covers the `__proto__` user, the sentinel log test, the forced 500 and the restart tests, and also NFR3.

Q1–Q3 from the brief are still open and none blocks this gate. The design asks for no requirement changes.

The gate command named `design.md`, so I used that path instead of the `spec.md` path in my role instructions.
